.TH PLMC 1 2026-10-26 plm09 "plm09 Manual"
.SH NAME
plmc \- PL/M-80 compiler for the Motorola 6809
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B plmc
.RB [ \-o
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-I
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-\-org
.IR addr ]
.RB [ \-\-vectors ]
.I file.plm
.br
.B plmc \-\-os9
.RB [ \-o
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-\-stack
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-\-name
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-\-memory
.IR n ]
.I file.plm
.br
.B plmc
.BR \-\-tokens | \-\-ast
.I file.plm
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B plmc
compiles a PL/M-80 program into 6809 assembly language for
.BR lwasm (1).
It implements PL/M-80 as described in Intel's
.I PL/M-80 Programming Manual
(9800268B): BYTE and ADDRESS variables, arrays, structures, BASED
variables, INITIAL and DATA, LITERALLY, procedures (including REENTRANT
and INTERRUPT procedures), labels and GOTO, the built-in procedures and
predeclared variables except INPUT and OUTPUT (the 6809 has no I/O
ports), and the
.B $INCLUDE
control: a line
.BI $INCLUDE( file )
with the
.B $
in column 1 compiles
.IR file 's
text in its place.
The file is looked for in the including file's directory, then in the
.B \-I
directories, as written and in lower case.
.PP
The output does not depend on the machine it will run on.
Everything machine-specific is in a hooks file,
.IR plm_hooks.asm ,
which lwasm finds through its include path, together with the startup
code and runtime routines, in
.\" installed-paths
.\" .IR @RUNTIME@ .
.\" source-paths
the
.I runtime
directory of the plm09 source tree, installed in
.IR DATADIR /plm09/runtime
.RI ( /usr/local/share/plm09/runtime
by default).
.\" /source-paths
To assemble the output:
.PP
.RS
.EX
lwasm \-9 \-f srec \-I runtime \-I runtime/\fItarget\fP \-o prog.s19 prog.asm
.EE
.RE
.PP
where
.I target
is one of the hooks directories:
.TP
.B sim
the
.BR sim6809 (1)
simulator
.TP
.B coco
the Tandy Color Computer under Color BASIC
.RB ( "\-\-org 3000H" ,
.B "lwasm \-f decb"
for a .BIN file)
.TP
.B os9
OS-9 and NitrOS-9 program modules (with
.BR \-\-os9 ,
and
.BR "lwasm \-f os9" )
.TP
.B flex
FLEX 9 commands (convert the S-records with
.BR srec2flex (1))
.TP
.B simon6809
the Simon6809 board, under its BUGGY monitor
.RB ( "\-\-org 0400H" )
.TP
.B kswichit
Wichit Sirichote's 6809 Microprocessor Kit
.RB ( "\-\-org 0200H" )
.TP
.B searle6809
Grant Searle's 6809 SBC, under ASSIST09 or BASIC
.RB ( "\-\-org 1000H" )
.TP
.B example
a template for a board with an MC6850 ACIA
.PP
Always pass
.B \-9
to lwasm, which otherwise accepts Hitachi 6309 instructions.
.SH OPTIONS
Numbers may be written in PL/M style
.RB ( 2000H ),
with
.B $
or
.B 0x
for hexadecimal, or in decimal.
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
Write the assembly to
.I file
instead of standard output.
.TP
.BI \-I " dir"
Also look for
.B $INCLUDE
files in
.IR dir ,
after the directory of the including file.
May be given more than once.
.TP
.BI \-\-org " addr"
Load and start address of the program (default $0100).
.TP
.B \-\-vectors
Also emit the 6809 reset vector at $FFFE, and the IRQ, FIRQ and NMI
vectors of any INTERRUPT procedures, for programs that run from ROM.
.TP
.B \-\-os9
Generate a position-independent, reentrant OS-9 program module.
Variables are kept in the process's data area and addressed through U.
.TP
.BI \-\-stack " n"
With
.BR \-\-os9 :
bytes of stack to reserve in the data area (default 256, at least 32).
.TP
.BI \-\-name " name"
With
.BR \-\-os9 :
the module name (default: the program's name).
.TP
.BI \-\-memory " n"
With
.BR \-\-os9 :
bytes of the data area for the predeclared array MEMORY (default 0).
.TP
.B \-\-tokens
Print the program's tokens and stop.
.TP
.B \-\-ast
Print the syntax tree, after LITERALLY substitution, and stop.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Errors and warnings are printed as
.IP
.IB file : line : column ": error: " message
.PP
and compilation stops at the first error.
Warnings do not stop compilation or change the exit status.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The program was compiled (or the tokens or tree printed).
.TP
.B 1
An error in the program, or a file that couldn't be read or written.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Compile, assemble and run a program in the simulator:
.PP
.RS
.EX
plmc \-o sum.asm sum.plm
lwasm \-9 \-f srec \-I runtime \-I runtime/sim \-o sum.s19 sum.asm
sim6809 sum.s19
.EE
.RE
.PP
Build an OS-9 module:
.PP
.RS
.EX
plmc \-\-os9 \-\-name sum \-o sum.asm sum.plm
lwasm \-9 \-f os9 \-I runtime \-I runtime/os9 \-o sum sum.asm
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sim6809 (1),
.BR srec2flex (1),
.BR lwasm (1)
.PP
The plmc manual,
.I docs/plmc.pdf
in the plm09 source tree, describes the language, the hooks and the
generated code in detail.
